{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/homogeneous-function.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/homogeneous-function.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does it mean for $\\boldsymbol{f}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^k$ to be homogeneous of degree $m$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{f}(cx_1,\\dots,cx_n) = c^m\\boldsymbol{f}(x_1,\\dots,x_n)$ for every point and $c\\ \\neq 0$.","$\\boldsymbol{f}(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = m$ for every point, universally and without exception.","$\\boldsymbol{f}$ has exactly $m$ input variables.","$\\boldsymbol{f}$ is always linear."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"738566249b38a18523f5d95d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a homogeneous function, which scalar values are allowed for $c$?","options":["Only $c = 0$.","All $c\\ \\neq 0$.","Only $c = 1$.","Only positive integers $c$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"60e2c0d230ae418e16989e25"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is homogeneous?","options":["$g(x,y) = xy+x$","$h(x,y) = x+y+1$","$p(x,y) = x^2+y$","$f(x,y) = x^2+y^2+xy$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"42c1847ee1c12f02a29532d0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2+xy$, what is $f(cx,cy)$?","options":["$cf(x,y)$","$c^3f(x,y)$","$c^2f(x,y)$","$f(x,y)+c$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66fa0c70b0b76f6e8c05ee80"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f$ is differentiable and homogeneous of degree $m$, what is true about each partial derivative?","options":["It is homogeneous of degree $m+1$.","It is always zero.","It is homogeneous of degree $m-1$.","It is always homogeneous of degree $m$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b2a9703d833ee85671d09812"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which operations preserve homogeneity?","options":["Only addition by constants.","Only taking absolute values.","Addition, multiplication, and composition.","Only changing the codomain dimension."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1752c59908d4b666e5a4438b"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Why is $g(x,y) = xy+x$ described as inhomogeneous?","options":["It has two input variables, irrespective of the particular case.","The terms scale with different powers of $c$.","It maps into $\\mathbb{R}$.","It contains a product term."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c3a314d1e307d91d95fe5184"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f$ is differentiable and homogeneous of degree $3$, what degree does $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_i}$ have?","options":["$3$","$4$","$0$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"768cc72c3ad93d63fcb44a97"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is simultaneous scaling of all arguments central to homogeneity?","options":["It studies changing only one coordinate while fixing the others.","The definition compares $\\boldsymbol{f}(cx_1,\\dots,cx_n)$ with $\\boldsymbol{f}(x_1,\\dots,x_n)$.","It studies adding the same constant to each coordinate.","It studies replacing the output by a scalar independent of the input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c40b2ce7d52678c8cff3988d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the degree $m$ measure for a homogeneous function?","options":["The number of variables in the domain.","The number of components in the codomain.","The power of $c$ by which the output scales.","The number of non-zero terms in the formula."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"86d349c1d1a05ec87ab0a287"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ and $g$ be homogeneous of degree $2$. What can be safely concluded about $f+g$?","options":["It is homogeneous of degree $4$.","It is homogeneous of degree $2$.","It is never homogeneous.","It is homogeneous only if $f = g$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f27cdaf0c37170667812a167"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ be homogeneous of degree $2$ and $g$ homogeneous of degree $3$. What degree is expected for the product $fg$?","options":["$5$","$6$","$1$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f24b2bd49db5ee35d126ca7"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For homogeneity, what is scaled by the same scalar $c$?","options":["Only the first input argument.","Only the output dimension.","Only the degree $m$.","All input arguments."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3e8c0a92e564deea569cb41c"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the output scaling rule for a homogeneous function of degree $m$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol f(c\\boldsymbol{x})=c^m\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol{x})$.","$\\boldsymbol f(c\\boldsymbol{x})=c+m+\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol{x})$.","$\\boldsymbol f(c\\boldsymbol{x})=\\boldsymbol f(\\boldsymbol{x})/c^m$.","$\\boldsymbol f(c\\boldsymbol{x})=0$ for every $c$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"da8efdbbb8f564a78a144bae"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If a function is homogeneous of degree $0$, how does its value change under simultaneous scaling?","options":["It stays the same.","It is multiplied by $c$.","It is multiplied by $c^2$.","It becomes undefined for all $c$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0d9426330156e3ca9f2971b6"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is homogeneous of degree $1$?","options":["$f(x,y)=xy+x$","$f(x,y)=x+y$","$f(x,y)=x^2+y+1$","$f(x,y)=\\sin x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"21e3565e345a611f5ee36064"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=xy$, what is $f(cx,cy)$?","options":["$cxy$","$xy+c$","$xy$","$c^2xy$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"36f0f790e65c2c4d309487be"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What degree of homogeneity does $f(x,y)=x^3$ have as a function on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?","options":["$3$","$2$","$1$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac26661433ce93a405f1c9ae"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens to the degree of a differentiable homogeneous function after taking a partial derivative?","options":["It increases by $1$.","It stays the same in every case.","It becomes undefined.","It decreases by $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a7e94bda96e0649fa9fa756a"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_homogeneous_function_ddce90f9_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f$ is differentiable and homogeneous of degree $5$, what degree is $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_i}$ expected to have?","options":["$5$","$6$","$4$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7568ff2872ae1f4ef01cbb23"}]}}